WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.110
- A few months ago I got an email
from a company called Sparkmate.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.250
They build prototypes.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.210
If you, or your company, has an idea
for a thing and the money to make it,

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.810
they will build that thing for you.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:12.530
They said to me, "Tom, we've got a bit
of spare time and want some publicity,

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.269
"do you have anything weird
that you want built?"

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:18.130
So I looked through the long list
of impractical projects

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.580
that are on my ideas board, and
I realized that I could answer a question

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.720
that I've had since I was very young.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.120
When I was a kid I watched a show called
Captain Scarlet and the Mysterons.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.660
It was a tale of good versus evil,
the heroic agents of Spectrum

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.870
fighting against the Mysterons from Mars.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.800
It was made with puppets,
the hero character was indestructible,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.410
which say brave narrative choice,

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.480
and most people remember
one thing about it:

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.906
the weird decision to do
big scene transitions like this.

00:00:43.906 --> 00:00:46.306
[loud drumbeats, timed with video cuts]

00:00:46.306 --> 00:00:49.220
Captain Scarlet was made in the '60s,
rerun in the '90s,

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.500
and kids like the young version of me
loved it.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.870
It turns out decades old shows
are still interesting

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.280
when you only have four TV channels
and no internet.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.650
Rewatching it as an adult for this video?

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.160
First, it's now in HD,
so you can see the puppet strings,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.030
and it is brutally violent
for a kid's show.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.890
In the first episode there's blood,
death by gunshot,

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.320
and an alien replicant blowing himself up,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.930
and the production didn't
shy away from that

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.060
by cutting to a reaction shot
of other characters.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:18.050
The special effects team literally put
explosives inside a puppet and blew it up.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.420
Wholesome family kids' entertainment there.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.200
And the plots often make no sense.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.310
I try to summarize the alien plan from
that first episode for this script,

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.270
and I just gave up.
It makes no sense and it keeps changing.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.220
No one in the entire episode,
human or alien, acts in any rational way.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.330
It's all just an excuse for cool
action scenes and explosions.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.660
And as a kid,
I didn't notice any of the plot holes,

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.110
because there were cool action scenes
and explosions.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.290
Plus the futuristic vehicles.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.350
The most famous of
which was the Spectrum Pursuit Vehicle,

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.310
the SPV.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.630
According to the lore of the
show it weighs eight tons,

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:53.850
but also somehow floats.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.620
Its top speed is 200 miles an hour,
and it comes equipped with

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.120
rocket cannons, ejector seats,
and a jet pack.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.180
Almost every episode,

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.050
a Spectrum agent would arrive at
some apparently normal location,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.029
and an SPV would be revealed
in an overdramatic way.

00:02:07.029 --> 00:02:10.140
The team at Sparkmate
have not made me an SPV.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.880
I'd love to have one,
but unfortunately this is reality,

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.160
but there's one other bizarre feature of
this thing

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.820
that always stuck with me as a kid.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:20.160
And it's introduced in that first episode
by a couple of lines of dialogue

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.330
that show just how far voice acting in kid
shows has improved since the '60s.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.880
- I see you have expert ratings on SPVs.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.260
[monotone] It must be tricky facing
backwards and driving by TV monitor.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.320
- Ah, you get used to it.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.430
- You drive the SPV facing
backwards using a screen.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.600
In the show, the explanation
for that is that it's safer.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.730
If you're in a crash you stand a better
chance of survival if you're facing backwards.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:41.790
That's true.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.530
There are real world studies
that show that,

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.440
but from a production perspective,
why would you do that?

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:47.460
It's not required for the plot.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.900
It just looks confusing to have
the background going the wrong way

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.360
and surely the characters
would get motion sick

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.520
and confused by how everything's turning.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.780
It's such a weird design choice.
It even shows up on the toy versions.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.141
If you open the doors here,

00:02:59.141 --> 00:03:02.250
the little action figures
are facing backwards.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.936
Even as a kid, I thought,
surely, that's not a good idea.

00:03:05.936 --> 00:03:10.140
So Sparkmate have made me a go-kart
that's driven backwards using a screen,

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.920
and that's literally all I know.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.440
I gave them that brief,
showed them Captain Scarlet episodes.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.480
They said, "That sounds fun,"
and now I'm sitting in their office

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.660
in an industrial park
on the outskirts of Paris

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.780
about to see the results
for the first time,

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.430
so I can answer the question I've had
since I was a kid.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.980
You can't actually drive facing backwards.
Right?

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.280
[loud drumbeats matching cuts]

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.460
Oh, wow.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:34.871
- Hope you like it!

00:03:34.871 --> 00:03:36.510
- This is the SPV, man!

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.450
- That's amazing.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:43.770
So you have a whole television
monitor there for all the details.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.130
Is that a... is that a game controller?
-Yes, it is.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:51.020
- Actually the first test that we did
was with this steering wheel and pedal,

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:56.270
and we decided to switch from
a steering wheel to an Xbox game pad.

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:56.730
Why?

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:01.230
Because the challenge for us
was to create a gamified experience

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.980
of driving a vehicle,
just like in a video game somehow.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.510
- And this camera...
- Is what you're seeing in the front.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:12.130
We even implemented some--
[honks horn]

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.520
- That is annoyingly loud and
I love it.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:17.980
I've got to remember
the cart is facing this way.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.430
Yes, let's take it out and see...

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.970
This feels way more dangerous
all of a sudden!

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.640
I also appreciate that this has
full harness seat belts.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.180
I thought we were going to make
a tiny little thing

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.720
that I'd sit on
and it would go at low speed.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.300
What's the top speed on this?

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:47.370
- In this area, we reached a 40km/h,
but with a higher distance, wider field,

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:51.960
we could reach 50-something, for sure.
- Wait. What's this for?

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:59.880
- This is a handbrake, emergency brake,
because sometimes things can happen!

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.769
For example, if we lose control,
or we just lose the controller.

00:05:04.769 --> 00:05:08.730
Brake, and that would be fine.
- So, low-speed test in the car park?

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.950
- Yes, that's fine.
- High-speed test on the track later.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.820
- Yes, go for that.
- Let's do it.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:17.880
- We thought about different positions,
we even thought of driving

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.680
laid at the bottom of the cart,
that was obviously a dangerous position,

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.230
so we went back to
a backward seated position.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:27.920
I would say it's just after some tests
with our team trying

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.140
what's the most comfortable...

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:35.869
I would say we tried and we ended up here
in this average position.

00:05:35.869 --> 00:05:38.890
- There we go. Avoid...
no, that's going for some cars.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:42.188
I'm 100% aiming for some cars.
Let's not do that.

00:05:42.188 --> 00:05:45.070
I'm aiming for the trucks
which are this way, here we go.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:46.430
- It's a bit confusing
at the beginning.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:49.090
-Oh, it's really confusing,
I don't like this!

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.670
-Try to be a bit slower
at the beginning,

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:56.960
because that's a bit messing
with your mind at first.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:57.790
- Agreed! It really is.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.880
- We actually used a
very powerful motor,

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.856
a powerful servo motor
to control the direction system.

00:06:04.856 --> 00:06:09.670
We went through a lot of iterations and
trials and error to configure the system.

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:12.360
Also on the mechanical part,
how do we install it,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.600
how do we control it
so that it's smooth?

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:19.500
That was a tricky part, I would say,
but once we had the propulsion system,

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.300
the direction system,
then we were good to go to have fun.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.880
- All right, let's go down the road.
I got-- oh, that's too fast.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.790
That's way too fast.
I nearly went the wrong way.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.520
I need to steer away from the van.
I nearly steered into the van.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.020
Got to remember the monitor--
- Keep your focus on the monitor.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.810
- Whoa, that was a drain,
I went straight over it!

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:40.600
Keep your focus on the monitor,
the monitor is all that counts.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:46.300
The minute I start thinking about
the real world outside the monitor,

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.200
my brain misfires, I get left and right-
oh, that was a speed bump-

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.430
I get left from right mixed up.
- You're driving pretty well.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:54.210
I'm very impressed.
- Let's bring it to a stop just here.

00:06:56.470 --> 00:07:01.410
It's possible, but for someone who hasn't
played video games in 15, 20 years,

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:07.490
I can do a lap around here,
it's just confusing to get used to it.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:12.170
It's just confusing that
the world is going one way,

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:14.160
but I have to focus everything
on the screen.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.660
- The visualization part,
we did a lot of iterations,

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:18.520
we did a lot of tests, a lot of coding,

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:22.860
and as you can imagine,
the main problem was latency.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.960
We tried different things,
but we always had to fight latency.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:35.010
We thought about drones, VR,
we ended up, I think the version that

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:39.280
we have right now is using WebRTC,
from a web browser communicating

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:41.380
through sockets to Unreal Engine.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:46.250
That's the most, I would say,
advanced we went on the VR part.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.280
- I asked the team for a steering wheel

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.650
and we have a hastily jerry-rigged
steering wheel here,

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:52.200
because it's just
a different type of controller.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.340
I've spent way more time with one of
these than I have with a game controller.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.730
You all ready?
All right, let's see how this...

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:05.320
Oh, that's nicer. Here we go. That's it.
Yep, okay, focus on screen. Anyone coming?

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:06.296
No one coming.

00:08:06.296 --> 00:08:11.680
We turn left out of here.
Slowly here, slow. That's good.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.240
You've got the sensitivity right now.
That's it. Here we go. A bit of speed.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:19.780
- Focus on the screen.
- Oh!

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:28.780
- What I did there was I turned right
when I should've turned left.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:30.840
You're okay?

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.810
-Wow! We're just fine, man.
We just avoided this steel thing.

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:40.350
- The good news is that the car works
and it goes at speed.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.410
-What happened? You were not
focused on the screen, no?

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:45.480
- I was very focused on the screen,
and all I needed to do was turn left,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.410
and my brain turned right.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:49.640
Wow, that's confusing.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.260
Well, I hate to say
"let's go to the track" on that,

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:55.380
but we should probably go to the track,

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.270
because I don't like the fact
that we're doing this around cars now.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.220
I've got lead foot.
I was so confident.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:04.900
- Fortunately, nothing bad happened.
- You built a strong kart there.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:06.770
- We just bumped,
we got scared, and that's it.

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:08.700
That's life.
- You built a strong kart, we're fine.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:11.400
Let's go to the track.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.461
[loud drumbeats, matching cuts]

00:09:14.461 --> 00:09:18.590
Power on, lights on,
little bit of acceleration.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:23.130
There we go.
That is twitchy. We're fine.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:25.360
Concentrate on the screen,
Tom, concentrate on the screen.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:29.900
Make it around the track.
There we go, there we go.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:30.700
Good.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.080
Little bit of straight here,
line it up there.

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:34.433
This is working.

00:09:34.433 --> 00:09:36.840
That's a little bit off course.
We're okay, we're good.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.140
Now we're on the straight.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.160
Floor it, let's see
what this thing can do.

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:45.050
Whoa! Okay.
That's fine, that's fine, that's fine.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:46.120
Brake.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:53.750
Yes, and right around here.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:09:59.740
Brake into the corner.

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:13.540
Wow, that's a strong left turn.

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:15.450
That was a strong left turn,
but I went the right way.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:16.270
The correct way.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:18.370
I didn't go the right way,
I went the correct way.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:25.130
Nearly.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:35.940
I can't believe I'm saying this,
but it works!

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:41.830
I was expecting to get motion sick.
I was expecting to fall over.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:45.850
I was expecting it to be like
"this old puppet show was terrible".

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:48.840
Yes, you can absolutely drive like that.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.060
My problems were sight lines
and not being used to the controls,

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:55.820
and this being a quickly jerry-rigged wheel.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:58.100
- Are you saying that
cars should be like this?

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:02.720
- I'm not saying it's an
improvement on driving forwards,

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:04.890
but if I'd crashed, I would've been safer!

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:10.230
[laughs]
It works. Thank you, folks.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.950
We've answered a question
I had for 25 years!

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:15.110
Thank you.

